        <h2>Brexit Changed Everything for UK Students</h2>
        <p>Before January 1, 2021, UK students could study in Spain freely as EU citizens. Post-Brexit, UK citizens now require a Spain Student Visa, just like American or Australian students. The process is straightforward, but costs have increased.</p>
      </div>

      <p>Spain remains a fantastic destination for British students. Spanish universities are excellent and affordable. The Student Visa process is simple, and the cost of living is lower than the UK. Many UK students studying here choose to stay after graduation.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Key Brexit Changes for UK Students</h2>

      <h3>Visas Now Required</h3>
      <p>Pre-Brexit: UK citizens could enroll without a visa, just like Spanish students. Post-Brexit: UK citizens must apply for a Spain Student Visa at Spanish embassies in the UK. Processing time: 4–8 weeks.</p>

      <h3>Tuition Rate Changes</h3>
      <p>Pre-Brexit: UK students paid EU rates (€1,500–€3,000/year at public universities). Post-Brexit: Most universities charge international rates (€6,000–€12,000/year) to UK students. This is a significant cost increase. Some regional universities or specific programs maintain lower rates; research thoroughly. Private universities remain expensive (€10,000–€25,000/year).</p>

      <h3>No Student Aid Eligibility</h3>
      <p>Pre-Brexit: UK students qualified for Spanish student grants and loans. Post-Brexit: Ineligible. Only Spanish residents and EU/EEA citizens qualify for most state aid. UK students must rely on personal funds, parental support, or UK government loans (which have limited coverage for studying abroad).</p>

      <h3>Healthcare Status</h3>
      <p>EHIC cards still work for emergency care, but Spain mandates private health insurance for visa approval. EHIC doesn't exempt you from needing private coverage.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Step-by-Step Visa Process for UK Citizens</h2>

      <h3>Step 1: Get Accepted to a Spanish University</h3>
      <p>Apply to universities normally. Get your enrollment letter on official institutional letterhead. This is your first key document.</p>

      <h3>Step 2: Gather Financial Proof</h3>
      <p>Show €1,050–€1,500/month available. Document this with UK bank statements (12 months), parental support letters (notarized), or other proof. UK sterling accounts are completely acceptable; no need for Spanish accounts yet.</p>

      <h3>Step 3: Obtain Health Insurance</h3>
      <p>Purchase private Spanish health insurance (€40–€100/month) or get proof from your university if included. EHIC is not sufficient for visa approval.</p>

      <h3>Step 4: Apply at Spanish Embassy</h3>
      <p>Identify the Spanish embassy or consulate in your region of the UK. Book an appointment and submit your application with: valid UK passport, enrollment letter, financial proof, health insurance documentation, criminal record clearance (PNC certificate from UK police), and completed visa application form. Pay the visa fee (typically £75–£150 depending on the embassy).</p>

      <h3>Step 5: Wait for Approval and Travel</h3>
      <p>Processing: 4–8 weeks. Once approved, travel to Spain with your visa. You must enter within the visa's 90-day validity window. Once in Spain, register locally (empadronamiento, police registration) to get your residency card (TIE).</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">UK Students: Financial Planning Post-Brexit</h2>

      <p><strong>Example cost for a UK student (one year):</strong></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Tuition at public university: €8,000</li>
        <li>Living costs (€1,200/month × 9 months academic year): €10,800</li>
        <li>Health insurance (€70/month × 12): €840</li>
        <li>Total year 1: €19,640</li>
      </ul>

      <p>This is still cheaper than UK university tuition alone (typically £9,000–£13,500/year) and far cheaper than private UK universities. Spain remains a cost-effective option despite post-Brexit increases.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Work Rights for UK Students</h2>

      <p>Same as all international students: 30 hours/week during the academic year, full-time during official holidays. Many UK students work part-time to offset costs. Popular jobs: English teaching (€15–€25/hour), hospitality (€10–€12/hour), summer tourism work. All work must be registered officially with Spanish Social Security.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Healthcare: EHIC vs Private Insurance</h2>

      <p><strong>EHIC (European Health Insurance Card):</strong> Still valid in Spain for emergency care. UK students can apply for or renew EHIC at NHS Choices website (free). Covers emergency healthcare, not routine care. <strong>Private insurance (required for visa):</strong> €40–€100/month. Many universities include it in enrollment fees. After you register in Spain, you can access Spain's public healthcare (SNS) free as a resident. Most UK students switch to SNS after registration.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Tax Obligations for UK Students in Spain</h2>

      <p>UK students studying in Spain must register with Spanish tax authorities if earning income above €15,000/year. Most student part-time work is below this threshold. You typically don't file Spanish tax returns unless you earn significant income. UK tax obligations depend on your residence status; consult a tax advisor if earning substantial income from work.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Returning to the UK After Studies</h2>

      <p>After your studies end, your Student Visa expires. Options: (1) Return to the UK, (2) Apply for another visa (Work Visa, Self-Employment Visa) if you want to stay, (3) Apply for Spanish permanent residency if you've been in Spain for 5+ years. Many UK graduates choose to transition to Spanish work visas if they find employment.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Common Mistakes UK Students Make Post-Brexit</h2>

      <p><strong>Assuming EHIC is sufficient healthcare:</strong> It's not. Spain mandates private insurance for visa approval. <strong>Not budgeting for international tuition rates:</strong> EU tuition is gone for UK students. Plan for €6,000–€12,000/year. <strong>Assuming UK student loans cover Spain:</strong> UK government loans have limitations for studying abroad. Check your loan provider's policies. <strong>Not registering after arrival:</strong> Registering locally is crucial. It gets you your residency card (TIE) and access to public healthcare. Don't delay this.</p>

      <h2 style="margin-top: 3rem;">Why UK Students Still Choose Spain</h2>

      <p>Despite post-Brexit changes, Spain remains attractive for UK students: (1) Lower overall cost than UK universities, (2) Excellent Spanish universities with English-taught programs, (3) Quality of life and climate, (4) Opportunity to learn/improve Spanish, (5) Gateway to European employment and residency. Thousands of UK students study in Spain annually and don't regret it.</p>
